### Describe the bug

Given a case like this, where `id` is `foo` on the server but `undefined` in the browser...

```svelte
<script>
  let { id } = $props();
</script>

<div id={id}>...</div>
```

...Svelte will render `<div id="foo">...</div>` on the server, but after hydration it will be `<div>...</div>` — as it should be.

If you do this instead...

```svelte
<div id={id}>...</div>
<div id={id}>...</div>
```

...the attributes will not be removed after hydration:

```html
<div id="foo">...</div>
<div id="foo">...</div>
```

The reason is that when there's a single update for a given block, we generate this...

```js
$.attr_effect(div, "id", () => $$props.id);
```

...but if we have multiple updates, we generate this:

```js
var div_id;
var div_1_id;

$.render_effect(() => {
  if (div_id !== (div_id = $$props.id)) {
    $.attr(div, "id", div_id);
  }

  if (div_1_id !== (div_1_id = $$props.id)) {
    $.attr(div_1, "id", div_1_id);
  }
});
```

In the latter case, `div_id` starts out as undefined, so `$.attr(...)` isn't called, and the attribute isn't removed.

There are two solutions that wouldn't involve significant changes. First, we could remove the `if` surrounding the `$.attr` calls (though this obviously results in false positives, particularly in cases like `aria-current={selected === item}`). Secondly, we could do `var div_id = $.UNINITIALIZED` instead, so that `$.attr` is always called. (At the same time, we should add the equality check to `$.attr_effect` and its various cousins.)

It might be worth considering more sweeping changes though. The distinction in `template.js` between `singular` and `grouped` updates is a source of very considerable complexity, and — as shown here — it creates space for bugs. And it saves us very little once the code is minified. I'm not sure the juice is worth the squeeze.

Perhaps a different approach is warranted. Today we have this:

```js
function attr_effect(dom, attribute, value) {
  render_effect(() => {
    attr(dom, attribute, value());
  });
}
```

If we add the equality check, it would become this:

```js
function attr_effect(dom, attribute, get_value) {
  let value = UNINITIALIZED;
  render_effect(() => {
    if (value === (value = get_value()) return;
    attr(dom, attribute, value);
  });
}
```

If instead of calling `render_effect`, we just returned the function...

```js
function attr_effect(dom, attribute, get_value) {
  let value = UNINITIALIZED;
  return () => {
    if (value === (value = get_value()) return;
    attr(dom, attribute, value);
  };
}
```

...then we could use it like this in the case where there's only one update, or we need to put it in its own render effect because it contains a call expression (or otherwise needs to be isolated from other render effects)...

```diff
-$.attr_effect(div, "id", () => $$props.id);
+$.render_effect($.attr_effect(div, "id", () => $$props.id));
```

...and in the case where there are multiple updates that can be optimized into a single render effect, then instead of this...

```js
var div_id = $.UNINITIALIZED;
var div_1_id = $.UNINITIALIZED;

$.render_effect(() => {
  if (div_id !== (div_id = $$props.id)) {
    $.attr(div, "id", div_id);
  }

  if (div_1_id !== (div_1_id = $$props.id)) {
    $.attr(div_1, "id", div_1_id);
  }
});
```

...we do this:

```js
var update_1 = $.attr_effect(div, "id", () => $$props.id);
var update_2 = $.attr_effect(div_1, "id", () => $$props.id);

$.render_effect(() => update_1(), update_2());
```

This means an extra three bytes in the first case (but this would be more than offset by the cases where there are multiple grouped updates), and extra function calls in the second. But it would increase consistency and remove a lot of complexity from the codebase.

